<!-- ===== SECTION 1: MODEL VERSIONING AND ARTIFACT TRACKING ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="model-versioning">
  <span class="section-label">Core Architecture</span>
  <h2>Model Versioning and Artifact Tracking</h2>

  <p>In production AI systems, reproducing a past prediction is as important as making accurate future ones. A customer asks: "Why did your model deny my loan application on March 15th?" You must be able to answer: "The loan model v2.3.1 was deployed on March 12th, trained on datasets X and Y with these hyperparameters, using code commit abc123def456." This level of traceability is impossible without disciplined artifact tracking.</p>

  <p>Model versioning extends beyond Git. You're versioning multiple dimensions simultaneously: the code that trains the model, the data used, the hyperparameters, the trained weights themselves, dependencies, and the evaluation metrics. Each dimension must be tracked independently and linked together.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A production model is like a laboratory experiment. The experiment is reproducible only if you document everything: which reagents you used (dataset versions), the exact procedure (code and commit hash), the equipment (framework versions), the date and time, and the results (metrics). Anyone with those details should be able to replicate your experiment exactly.</p>
  </div>

