<!-- ===== SECTION 2: REPRODUCIBLE ML PIPELINES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="reproducibility">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture Patterns</span>
  <h2>Reproducible ML Pipelines</h2>

  <p>Reproducibility means: given the same inputs, you get the same outputs. This sounds trivial, but it's surprisingly hard. Random seeds in training, floating-point arithmetic differences across platforms, non-deterministic GPU operations, and data loading order all introduce variability. In regulated industries, reproducibility is mandatory. In all industries, it's essential for debugging.</p>

  <p>Building reproducible pipelines requires discipline: seeding randomness, locking dependencies, documenting data preprocessing steps, and validating that the pipeline produces identical results when re-run.</p>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Healthcare AI:</strong> When a diagnostic model's prediction is challenged in court, you must prove the exact data and algorithm used. Reproducibility is a legal requirement.</p>
    <p><strong>In Finance:</strong> Regulators demand detailed audit trails. You must be able to explain and reproduce every model decision within 48 hours.</p>
    <p><strong>In Open Research:</strong> Published results that aren't reproducible damage scientific credibility and waste other researchers' time.</p>
  </div>

