  <h3>Risk-Based Governance Tiers</h3>
  <p>Not all models are equal. A model recommending blog articles has different risk than a model approving medical treatment. Your governance should be proportional:</p>

  <div class="compare-grid">
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>Tier 1: Low Risk</h5>
      <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Content recommendations, search ranking</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automated tests + 1 human review</li>
        <li>Can deploy to 10% canary in <2 hours</li>
        <li>Rollback authority: on-call engineer</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>Tier 2: Medium Risk</h5>
      <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Fraud detection, loan pricing</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automated tests + 2 expert reviews</li>
        <li>Canary requires 24hr monitoring window</li>
        <li>Rollback authority: product + engineering leads</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>Tier 3: High Risk</h5>
      <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Medical diagnosis, employment decisions</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automated tests + 3 reviews + legal</li>
        <li>Canary minimum 72 hours</li>
        <li>Rollback authority: executive approval required</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Document governance policies explicitly — no ad-hoc decisions</li>
      <li>Use risk-based tiers: high-stakes models need more oversight</li>
      <li>Automate checks where possible (tests, metrics, data quality)</li>
      <li>Require human review for approval gates (never fully automated)</li>
      <li>Define clear escalation paths when policies are violated</li>
      <li>Review and update policies annually as your systems evolve</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

