<!-- ===== SECTION 5: DISASTER RECOVERY AND ROLLBACK ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="disaster-recovery">
  <span class="section-label">Operational Resilience</span>
  <h2>Disaster Recovery and Rollback Procedures</h2>

  <p>Eventually, something will go wrong. A bad model gets deployed. A data quality issue corrupts predictions. An upstream system fails. Disasters are not a matter of if, but when. Your job is to make rollback fast, predictable, and low-risk.</p>

  <p>The key principle: make rollback a first-class operation, as important as deployment. Test rollback procedures. Document exactly who can execute them. Empower on-call engineers to make the decision to rollback without waiting for approvals.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine a highway bridge with a crack. You can't instantly repair it, but you absolutely can immediately reroute traffic. Similarly, when a model fails, you don't fix it in production — you immediately switch to the previous version (or a safe default) and investigate offline.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash (Rollback Script)</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm">#!/bin/bash</span>
<span class="cm"># Quick rollback to previous model version</span>

CURRENT_VERSION<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"v2.3.1"</span>
PREVIOUS_VERSION<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"v2.3.0"</span>
MODEL_NAME<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"loan-approval"</span>

<span class="cm"># 1. Immediately reduce traffic to zero</span>
kubectl <span class="fn">set</span> deployment/<span class="st">model-serving</span> \
  <span class="st">-c</span> <span class="st">model-serving</span> \
  <span class="st">-e MODEL_WEIGHT=0</span>

<span class="cm"># 2. Log the rollback event (immutable audit trail)</span>
<span class="fn">echo</span> <span class="st">"$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"</span> \
  <span class="st">"ROLLBACK: $CURRENT_VERSION to $PREVIOUS_VERSION"</span> \
  <span class="op">|</span> tee <span class="st">-a</span> /var/log/audit/rollback.log

<span class="cm"># 3. Update model serving endpoint</span>
kubectl patch service model-serving <span class="st">--type merge</span> \
  <span class="st">-p</span> <span class="st">'{...}'</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening:</strong> The rollback script first stops traffic to the bad model, logs the event to an immutable audit trail, then switches the serving endpoint to the previous version. This can complete in <30 seconds.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Production Systems:</strong> Every second a bad model serves predictions costs money and erodes customer trust. A rollback that takes 5 minutes beats a fix that takes 2 hours.</p>
    <p><strong>In Regulated Industries:</strong> Regulators care less about whether you had a problem than about how fast you detected and fixed it. Fast rollback demonstrates operational maturity.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Design rollback as a first-class operation, tested before you need it</li>
      <li>Keep previous model versions running in warm standby for <2min switch</li>
      <li>Define clear trigger conditions for automatic rollback (error rate, latency, data drift)</li>
      <li>Log all rollback events immutably for compliance</li>
      <li>Empower on-call engineers to execute rollback without permission chains</li>
      <li>Conduct monthly "chaos engineering" drills: practice rollback scenarios</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

