<!-- ===== SECTION 6: MODEL CARDS AND DOCUMENTATION ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="model-cards">
  <span class="section-label">Documentation Standards</span>
  <h2>Model Cards and Complete Provenance</h2>

  <p>A model card is a living document that travels with a model from training through production. It answers: What is this model for? What data was it trained on? What are its limitations? Under what conditions does it fail? Who should not use it? It's the difference between a model and a trustworthy model.</p>

  <p>Model cards originated in AI ethics research but have become industry standard. They serve machine learning practitioners, product managers, compliance officers, and users.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Model Card Template Structure</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Model Details</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Model name, version, date</li>
          <li>Model type (neural network, tree, etc.)</li>
          <li>Framework (TensorFlow, PyTorch)</li>
          <li>Training time and hardware</li>
          <li>Intended use: primary and secondary</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Data</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Training data description</li>
          <li>Data sources and collection date</li>
          <li>Dataset size and splits</li>
          <li>Preprocessing transformations</li>
          <li>Known data quality issues</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Performance</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Evaluation metrics (accuracy, AUC, F1)</li>
          <li>Performance by demographic group</li>
          <li>Comparison to baseline</li>
          <li>Metrics on holdout test set</li>
          <li>Known failure modes</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Limitations & Ethics</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Known limitations and biases</li>
          <li>Demographic disparity analysis</li>
          <li>When the model should NOT be used</li>
          <li>Recommended monitoring</li>
          <li>Ethical considerations</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Example: Loan Approval Model Card</h3>
  <p><strong>Model Version:</strong> loan-approval-v2.3.1 | <strong>Updated:</strong> 2026-03-15</p>
  <p><strong>Intended Use:</strong> Screen loan applications in the initial phase. Not intended as sole approval criteria. Requires human underwriter review for all approved applicants.</p>
  <p><strong>Known Limitations:</strong> Performance drops 8% for applicants with <1 year credit history. Model trained primarily on applicants 25-65 years old; extrapolation to other ages recommended. Downside: may reject borderline-good applicants (false negative rate 3.2%).</p>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Create a model card for every production model</li>
      <li>Include: intended use, training data, performance, and limitations</li>
      <li>Analyze demographic performance disparity explicitly</li>
      <li>Document when the model should NOT be used</li>
      <li>Update the card when model, data, or performance changes</li>
      <li>Share model cards with all stakeholders (product, compliance, users)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>Why is a model card important even when the model is working well?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) It helps stakeholders understand intended use and limitations upfront</li>
        <li>B) It makes the model perform better</li>
        <li>C) It reduces the need for monitoring</li>
        <li>D) It's only required for regulated industries</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">A model card communicates critical context: what the model can and cannot do, what data it was trained on, and under what conditions it was tested. This prevents misuse even when the model functions technically correctly.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

